<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Morley, 1st Viscount Morley of Blackburn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1838–1923</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as John Morley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Morley, 1st Viscount Morley of Blackburn, was a British Liberal politician, journalist and writer. A close ally and biographer of William Gladstone, he served as Chief Secretary for Ireland and, from 1905 to 1910, as Secretary of State for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, when with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> Lord Minto he introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-councils-act-1909">Morley–Minto reforms</a> of 1909. He resigned from the cabinet in 1914 in protest against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-morley-1st-viscount-morley-of-blackburn.jpg" alt="John Morley, 1st Viscount Morley of Blackburn" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W. &amp;amp; D. Downey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morley-John-Viscount.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
